// =============================================================================
// DevOps Portal — Tailscale Funnel SINGLE-SLOT TCP passthrough config.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this file looks so different from the previous (3-slot HTTPS) version:
//
//   * Tailscale Funnel free-tier caps public HTTPS services at 3 host:port
//     tuples. The previous design used all 3 (portal apex + grafana + gitlab)
//     and marked the other 30 tools "tailnet-only". The user now wants ALL
//     tools publicly reachable on *.deepaksharma.live.
//
//   * A single `tailscale funnel` entry in TCP passthrough mode on port 443
//     counts as ONE service, regardless of how many SNI hostnames flow
//     through it. This is the loophole we exploit to expose 28+ tools
//     on one funnel slot.
//
//   * Tailscale's built-in HTTPS cert is only issued for the tailnet MagicDNS
//     name (`*.deepak-desktop.tailac51e7.ts.net`). Serving `*.deepaksharma.live`
//     therefore requires OUR OWN Let's Encrypt wildcard cert, terminated
//     at host-side nginx on port 8443 (see nginx-proxy/portal.conf).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Format: legacy TCP/Web/AllowFunnel huJSON. Verified against:
//   * https://tailscale.com/kb/1247/funnel  ("Tailscale Funnel")
//   * https://tailscale.com/kb/1242/tailscale-serve  ("tailscale serve")
//   * `tailscale serve --help` / `tailscale funnel --help` output for 1.84.x.
//
// TCP block semantics (verified against docs above):
//   * TCP["443"].HTTPS:true       — this is a TLS listener; Funnel requires it
//                                   for public ingress on 443.
//   * TCP["443"].TCPForward       — raw TCP forward target; the bytes on the
//                                   wire are NOT decrypted by tailscaled.
//   * TCP["443"].TerminateTLS:""  — explicit sentinel meaning "DO NOT terminate
//                                   TLS at tailscaled; the downstream target
//                                   (host nginx on :8443) will terminate."
//                                   Empty string = no SNI restriction, so ALL
//                                   SNIs flowing through the funnel pass through.
//
// Allowed funnel ports on free tier (verified at
// https://tailscale.com/kb/1223/funnel-availability#funnel-ports):
//   * 443, 8443, 10000
//   Port 443 is the only one that doesn't require end-users to include the
//   port in the URL — so that's the one we use. `bootstrap-tcp.sh --apply`
//   will explicitly `tailscale funnel --bg 443` to enable it.
//
// Forward target port selection:
//   * Tailscaled owns :443 on the tailnet side (public). It cannot also bind
//     :443 on the loopback (would conflict). So host nginx binds :8443 and we
//     forward to 127.0.0.1:8443.
//
// MagicDNS placeholder:
//   The bootstrap script substitutes ${MAGIC_DNS_NAME} at runtime using
//   `tailscale status --json | jq -r .Self.DNSName | sed 's/\.$//'`.
//   NEVER hardcode the tailnet name in git; it's specific to the account
//   and can leak personal info.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// huJSON specifics (https://github.com/tailscale/hujson):
//   * Comments (// and /* */) are permitted.
//   * Trailing commas are permitted.
//   * `tailscale serve set-config` round-trips this into the daemon as JSON.
//
// Do not include real tokens, cert material, or API keys anywhere in this
// file — tailscaled writes this to disk verbatim under /var/lib/tailscale.
// =============================================================================
{
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // TCP block — single TLS listener on :443, pass-through to host nginx.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "TCP": {
    "443": {
      "HTTPS": true,
      "TCPForward": "127.0.0.1:8443",
      "TerminateTLS": ""
    }
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // AllowFunnel — only the MagicDNS :443 tuple is public.
  // Exactly ONE host:port entry here => consumes 1 of 3 funnel slots.
  //
  // IMPORTANT: for TCP passthrough, AllowFunnel is keyed on the node's
  // MagicDNS name (Self.DNSName without trailing dot). Every SNI the client
  // presents at the TLS layer is simply relayed to 127.0.0.1:8443 — nginx
  // handles SNI dispatch to the right backend.
  //
  // bootstrap-tcp.sh substitutes ${MAGIC_DNS_NAME} from `tailscale status --json`.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "AllowFunnel": {
    "${MAGIC_DNS_NAME}:443": true,
  },
}
